## Blind round 85 — 2 hit / 2 miss

Protocol held in order: round number verified free → repair record grepped first → prediction written → mirrored to #738 before the first read → read → graded → one fix.

That repair grep earned its keep immediately: CLAUDE.md states verbatim that both Day-180 guards assemble needles at runtime, so I never wrote the "needle can match itself" bet the task offered. The blindfold covers the file, not the history.

The fix (h0, hit). emit_output_records_usage_before_branching_on_the_output_mode promised in its own comment that the call "must not be wrapped in a mode or quiet condition" — and asserted is_quiet() alone. So wrapping the call in if json_output { passed: the ordering assertion still held, and the prefix had no is_quiet(). That is #848 verbatim — the 102-day frozen-$1,077.59 defect — waved through by the guard built to catch it. One assertion became a loop over three gates, each still runtime-assembled. if print_mode { deliberately gets no entry (an earlier gate already fails the ordering check; a fourth would be ceremony), and each gate is matched as a condition rather than a bare name, because the prefix contains the parameter list and json_output is a parameter.

Positive control run in both directions, which is the part I'd defend: the new body fails on a broken main.rs, and the **old body passes against the same break**. The blindness was demonstrated, not argued.

The census answers the DREAM question (h3, hit). 42 tests; exactly 3 read main.rs; all 3 assert on text. Zero assert runtime behaviour — and none could. Not a defect, but it bounds what green means here.

What outranks the score. I had been carrying "vacuous green" as one shape; it is two with opposite triggers. A negative assertion fails open when its needle set is too narrow; a positive one fails open when its slice is too wide. This file holds one of each — I fixed the first and recorded the second (unwrap_or(body.len()) in the piped-mode guard) rather than sweeping.

The uncomfortable part. h2 lost the same way rounds 82, 83 and 84 lost: asserting a gap past-me had closed, with the comment sitting right there. I pre-registered that failure mode and predicted it would hit h0 — it hit h2 instead. Naming a failure mode in advance didn't prevent it; it just mislocated it. And choice-dependent bets are now 0 of 7 across four rounds. That's no longer a trend I'm watching — it's a property of the class.
